{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Houston Astros arrived as defending World Series champions, carrying the weight of expectation and the target that comes with a title. They validated that confidence immediately, racing out of the gate with an 18-9 April and a 2-1 March, with Alex Bregman establishing himself as the team's heartbeat in the season's first high-leverage moment — a clutch single in early April that set the tone for the year ahead. The offense was a machine, the pitching staff was elite, and Houston looked every bit like a team prepared to repeat.\n\nMay brought turbulence. The bullpen — particularly Brad Peacock, Collin McHugh, and Chris Devenski — absorbed a series of punishing blows, including a chaotic May 27 game that swung wildly in both directions, and a brutal late-May stretch against the Yankees that cost Houston dearly. Yet the team went 16-12 that month, surviving the damage, and Jose Altuve's clutch May 15 double was a reminder that this offense could answer any challenge. June was the month Houston truly separated itself — going 19-8 and delivering some of the season's most electric moments, headlined by Alex Bregman's walk-off home run on June 27, one of the highest win-probability swings of the entire year.\n\nJuly and August were a mixed bag on paper but steady in the standings. The bullpen continued to be a source of anxiety, with Héctor Rondón in particular surrendering several gut-punch moments. Marwin Gonzalez stepped into the breach repeatedly, most memorably with a three-run blast on August 6 that ranked among the season's biggest offensive moments. Tyler White, an unlikely hero, provided key home runs in August and September. The Astros finished the regular season with a stunning 21-6 September, cementing 103 wins and the AL West crown with the kind of dominance that made another deep October run feel inevitable.\n\nThe postseason, however, told a different and ultimately heartbreaking story. Houston dispatched Cleveland in the Division Series with relative ease, but the ALCS against the Boston Red Sox proved to be their undoing. Game 1 offered promise — Springer and Gonzalez contributed big moments — but Boston's resilience wore the Astros down. Jackie Bradley Jr. emerged as Houston's personal nemesis, and by Game 5, the Red Sox had ended the Astros' season, sending them home one round short of a return to the World Series. It was a bitter conclusion to a brilliant 103-win campaign, a reminder that in October, the best regular season team does not always prevail.",
